  "tree_id": 175,
  "family": "Ericaceae",
  "memo": "Madrone is an iconic evergreen tree native to the Pacific Coast of North America, ranging from Southern California to Vancouver and inland through the Cascade Range and Sierra Nevada. It occurs in patchy stands in dry, rocky soils, on coastal bluffs, and in mixed evergreen forests and woodlands. Of the approximately twenty species in the genus &lt;em&gt;Arbutus&lt;/em&gt;, only &lt;em&gt;A. menziesii&lt;/em&gt; is native to California.&lt;br&gt;Madrone is easily recognized by its smooth, orangish-red bark, which exfoliates in thin, curly sheets to reveal a cool, greenish layer beneath, as well as its glossy, two-toned leaves. In spring, small, white, lantern-shaped flowers emerge and develop into red, warty fruits when pollinated. Beyond its ecological role, madrone is also cultivated as an ornamental tree. It grows best in full sun and well-draining soils with minimal summer water.",
