  "ethnobotanical_info": "Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest used madrone bark and leaves to prepare medicines for a variety of ailments. The wood was used to make small tools, while the berries were sometimes eaten fresh or used to prepare beverages. The tree also holds cultural significance, with regional stories and traditions connecting madrone to the people who have historically lived in the landscapes where it grows.",
